{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon CloudFront announces mutual TLS support for origins - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudFront has introduced mutual TLS (mTLS) support for origins, allowing customers to authenticate requests from their CloudFront distributions to origin servers using TLS certificates. This eliminates the need for custom security controls and reduces operational overhead. Origin mTLS is supported for origins on AWS, such as Application Load Balancer and API Gateway, as well as on-premises and custom origins. Customers can use certificates issued by AWS Private Certificate Authority or third-party private Certificate Authorities, which they import through AWS Certificate Manager. Origin mTLS is available at no extra cost and is supported in the Business and Premium pricing plans. Customers can configure origin mTLS using various methods, including the AWS Management Console, CLI, SDK, CDK, or CloudFormation. Origin mTLS provides cryptographic verification of CloudFront's identity and ensures that only verified CloudFront distributions can connect to backend infrastructure","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/amazon-cloudfront-mutual-tls-for-origins/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"What's New at AWS - Cloud Innovation & News","context":"Amazon CloudFront has introduced two new security features to enhance encryption and protect against future quantum computing threats. The first feature is hybrid post-quantum key establishment support across all existing TLS security policies, which provides future-proof encryption for client-to-edge connections. This feature is automatically enabled for all existing security policies at no additional cost. The second feature is a new TLS 1.3 only security policy (TLS1.3_2025), which allows customers to leverage the latest TLS protocol for improved security and performance. This policy is also available at no extra charge and is compatible with all CloudFront edge locations. These updates enable organizations to meet specific security and compliance requirements while ensuring long-term data security and regulatory compliance readiness. Both features are quantum-resistant and do not incur any additional charges. For more information on implementing these new security policies in CloudFront distributions, visit the CloudFront documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/09/amazon-cloudfront-TLS-policy-post-quantum-support/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon CloudFront now supports TLS 1.3 for origin connections - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudFront has implemented support for Transport Layer Security (TLS) 1.3 when connecting to origins, enhancing security and improving performance for origin communications. This upgrade offers stronger encryption algorithms, reduced handshake latency, and a better overall security posture for data transmission between CloudFront edge locations and origin servers. TLS 1.3 is automatically enabled for all origin types, including custom origins, Amazon S3, and Application Load Balancers, with no configuration changes required. TLS 1.3 provides faster connection establishment through a reduced number of round trips during the handshake process, delivering up to 30% improvement in connection performance when the origin supports it. CloudFront will automatically negotiate TLS 1.3 when the origin supports it, while maintaining backward compatibility with lower TLS versions for origins that haven't yet upgraded. This enhancement benefits applications requiring high security standards, such as financial services, healthcare, and e-commerce platforms that handle sensitive data. TLS 1.3 support for origin connections is available at no additional charge in all CloudFront edge locations. For more information about CloudFront origin TLS, refer to the Amazon CloudFront Developer Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-cloudfront-tls13-origin/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"cloudfront","skill_description":"Configures Amazon CloudFront content delivery across six workflows: when to use CloudFront and how it fits with AWS WAF, Shield, CloudFront Functions, Lambda@Edge, Route 53, and origins (creating a distribution, caching, and Flat Rate Pricing (FRP) versus pay-as-you-go pricing); managing custom-domain TLS certificates (ACM in us-east-1); configuring multi-tenant distributions; protecting origins with origin access control (OAC), VPC origins, and origin mutual TLS (mTLS); securing content with signed URLs and cookies, geographic restrictions, viewer mutual TLS, and edge token validation; and observing traffic with standard and real-time logs. Applicable when the customer wants to put CloudFront in front of content, choose pricing, lock an origin, restrict who can view content, or analyze logs. Not applicable for the Route 53 DNS side of a CloudFront custom domain or failover between distributions, or for pure-Route 53 DNS work (see the route53 skill).\n\n\nServices: cloudfront, s3, acm, vpc\nTasks: configure, secure, deploy\nPersona: developer, devops, sre\nWorkload: networking, content-delivery, security","skill_name":"cloudfront"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-security","skill_description":"Covers AWS security services and workflows — Security Hub V2 (OCSF) findings, connectors, aggregators, automation rules, and security posture summaries; Security Hub CSPM (V1/ASFF) controls and compliance standards; GuardDuty threat findings; Inspector vulnerability findings; Macie sensitive data findings; Detective investigation; and Security Lake configuration and data aggregation. Applicable when questions involve security posture, Exposure findings, CSPM failed controls, threat findings, vulnerability findings, sensitive data findings, automation rules, or cross-service security configuration across AWS environments. Procedures use standard AWS CLI syntax and work with or without the AWS MCP server.\n\nServices: guardduty, inspector2, securityhub, macie2, detective, securitylake\nTasks: review, summarize, configure\nPersona: security-engineer, cloud-architect\nWorkload: security","skill_name":"aws-security"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudFront default certificate forces minimum TLS protocol to TLSv1", "parent_id": "9fd015ff-eb74-4c4b-b2b6-2c5aab2e4347"}

